/**

@page page_exahype_amr Adaptive mesh refinement

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE solvers return cell-wise refinement decisions. The generated action
sets turn these decisions into peano4::grid::GridControlEvent records, and
exahype2::RefinementControlService gives every rank the same canonical request
set.

# Enabling adaptive levels

The project configuration supplies a regular base level and a maximum level.
Adaptivity is active when the maximum exceeds the base. Each additional level
divides the cell width by the subdivision factor of the active curve.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
exahype2Project = exahype2.Project(
  ...,
  min_grid_levels=baseLevels,
  max_grid_levels=maximumLevels,
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The solver installs its criterion through `set_pde_terms()`:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver.set_pde_terms(
  ...,
  refinement_criterion=True,
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The generated solver expects a `refinementCriterion()` implementation that
returns exahype2::RefinementCommand::Refine,
exahype2::RefinementCommand::Keep, or
exahype2::RefinementCommand::Erase.

# Decision semantics

`Refine` names the current cell and requests one finer neighbourhood around
it. The configured maximum level suppresses requests from cells that are
already sufficiently fine. `Keep` emits no structural request. `Erase`
contributes one vote towards removing the current cell's sibling group.

Erasure requires agreement from all children of the parent and all
participating solvers. The service promotes a complete sibling vote to the
parent cell. Peano then removes the associated plan flags only when the whole
adjacent parent-cell ring agrees and no refine request protects it.

# Distributed requests

Each rank evaluates the criterion on its owned cells. At the end of the step,
the refinement service gathers, sorts, and deduplicates the committed records.
All ranks then evaluate the same vector and independently derive the same
target tree, ownership table, boundary topology, and migration.

A solver that participates in the refinement service contributes its decision
for every evaluated cell. Omitting a solver's erase vote prevents the sibling
consensus required for coarsening.

# Regrid drains

The time loop invokes the dynamic-AMR phase after a step commits requests. One
sweep triggers and prepares a changed plan; the transition sweep emits the
create and destroy callbacks and migrates the persistent solver pools. The
phase continues until the topology reports the required stationary sweeps.

A criterion can evaluate only cells that exist. Refinement through several
additional levels therefore requires a new decision after each level appears.
The time loop resumes only after the complete drain settles.

# Solver data transfer

Interpolation initialises data created on a finer level. Restriction preserves
fine-grid information when cells disappear or when coarse and fine regions
exchange face data. Each solver family owns the interpolation, restriction,
halo, and time-stepping policy required by its data layout.

Finite-volume solvers select these operators through their solver
configuration. Wider halos require an operator that defines every coarse and
fine halo value available at an AMR interface.

*/
